   "load1": null,
   "note": "Inherited from the flat tests/durations.json as it stood at d49964e. No run recorded itself, so when, at what ref, at what worker count and under what load these were taken is not known and is not guessed here. Two independent reasons to distrust them as a set: tests/parallel's own docstring says they were taken under foreign load and are 3-4x too LARGE, while test_header_rule_harness.py is 10x too SMALL (25.53 recorded; 265.996s and 260.74s measured at d49964e, the very commit that wrote this file). Both cannot be true of one run. Their serial total is 1968.9s against a measured 162.4s at 8 workers = 1299 worker-seconds, so as a set they describe no run that has happened. Retained anyway: TASK-230 measured that even these produce a schedule equal to the perfect-knowledge one in three runs of four. A hint may be wrong and still useful; it may not be wrong and silent. Replace with `python3 tests/parallel --record` on a quiet machine."
  },
  "never-measured": {
   "ref": null,
   "taken": null,
   "workers": null,
   "load1": null,
   "note": "Listed with no number because none has ever been taken. sec:null sorts as inf, so these still run FIRST - which is what an unmentioned module already did, now by declaration instead of by omission. Being listed is what lets the drift check tell 'nobody has measured this yet' apart from 'somebody deleted the entry'; those were the same thing before TASK-304."
  },
  "task-067-one-choke-point": {
   "ref": "47ffa26",
   "taken": "2026-09-03",
   "workers": 1,
   "load1": 3.16,
   "note": "Measured, not inherited and not guessed. Three serial runs of tests.test_one_choke_point with __pycache__ cleared before each — 1.49 / 1.54 / 1.52s, median 1.52 — at load1 3.16 on the machine that ran the TASK-067 suite the same hour. Serial rather than under the parallel runner on purpose: a 1.5s module's figure is a sort key and a worker-contended number would be a worse one. The spread is quoted whole rather than best-case, as tests/parallel's own docstring asks."
  }
